04, 45.673, 45.755, 45.801, 45.848, 45.917, 45.952, 45.999, 46.033, 46.126, 46.219, 46.277, 46.324, 46.393, 46.44, 46.475, 46.591, 46.742, 46.892, 46.962, 47.043, 47.125, 47.171, 47.229, 47.299, 47.357, 47.473, 47.589, 47.74, 47.833, 47.926, 47.984, 48.019, 48.1, 48.158, 48.251, 48.309, 48.483, 48.506, 48.715]}, "quality_check": null, "script_sha256": "cc953865fd69de118c0029f70d22f8ee21fb6688e18e5eb1f1c416312666f31f", "text": "Look at Klopp at the end of that 2019 season. Still applauding. Still trying to give the supporters something. That is part of a manager's job, but imagine having to do it after taking a team that close. Ninety-seven points should have felt enormous. Instead, the first question was what Liverpool could have done differently. Another draw turned into a win. Another chance taken. Another tiny improvement squeezed out of a team that had already lost only once. Yes, that defeat came against City. Yes, Liverpool had chances to change their own story. But I hate the way finishing second can shrink an extraordinary achievement into a failure. Klopp had built a team capable of a championship season. The table said it still wasn't enough. And the next demand was obvious. Go again. Find more.", "voice_id": "bu5eKETbFKC8G702EAU4", "model_id": "eleven_multilingual_v2"}